{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://aureline.dev/schemas/collaboration/m5-shared-terminal-debug-view-and-control-channel-badge-registries.schema.json",
  "title": "Aureline M5 shared-terminal-debug-view and control-channel-badge registries",
  "description": "Export-safe packet for the shared-terminal-debug-view and control-channel-badge lane over the frozen M5 collaboration-control component matrix (m5_shared_terminal_debug_control_grant_presenter_consent_retention_and_session_restore_view_matrix). It makes the read-only shared terminal / debugger view stream and the control-channel badge operable by carrying resolved, honest projections of two registries so the claimed M5 shared terminal / debug view, companion-follow, control-grant prompt, paste / secret guard, support / export, and help / docs surfaces inherit one canonical view-stream descriptor and one control-channel badge rather than a hand-authored parallel prose that has to be kept consistent. Registry-A resolves the shared-terminal-debug view stream (session start / stop identity, surface type, target context, participant scope and observing roles, command or frame markers, the read-only default a session begins in, and the control-channel state kept distinct from text / presence channels). Registry-B resolves the control-channel badge (whether input authority is unavailable, requestable, granted to a single driver, or expired, plus the fresh visible authority event any control request, grant, expiry, or presence reconnect / cursor-follow / companion resume on an already-active session must raise). Each registry row binds a consumer surface to resolved view-stream entries and resolved control-channel-badge entries. Hard invariants forbid a clean projection from reading as trustworthy while collaboration presence implicitly acquires terminal / debug control, more than one active driver holds a sensitive surface, recording / retention / guest-scope widening / route-visibility expansion starts silently, prior terminal / debug input replays on join or restore, or raw secrets / command text / variable bodies / clipboard contents are revealed without an explicit policy / consent posture and visible guardrail. Acceptance criteria (viewers can observe terminal or debugger state without inheriting input authority or secret visibility; and active badges identify surface type, target context, and whether control is unavailable, requestable, granted, or expired) are proven by the resolved examples, not merely asserted. Raw secrets, raw command text, variable bodies, clipboard contents, and private endpoints never cross this boundary. The Rust validator in crates/aureline-ui is the authoritative gate; this schema documents the shape.",
